---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import d from "../data/sneyds.json";
const c = d.counts;
---
<Base
  title="The Sneyds of the Potteries"
  eyebrow={`${c.records} records · ${c.families} households · searched ${d.searched}`}
  dek="Every Staffordshire Sneyd record, assembled into households — which finally produces the family the tree has been pointing at, and moves one of its dates by twelve years.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">This morning this archive reported the Sneyd line unevidenced. By evening it had
    found two of its four links. Reconstituting the county — all <strong>{c.records}</strong> records
    read individually and assembled into <strong>{c.families}</strong> households — produces the
    third, and corrects the tree in the process.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Madeley All Saints</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">William Sneyd, weaver, and his thirteen children</h2>

    <p>On <strong>27 March 1769</strong> William Sneyd, <strong>weaver</strong>, married Mary
    Blackbourne at Madeley, witnessed by Robert Sneyd. <a href={u("/sneyd/")}>That record →</a> Here
    is what follows it in the same register:</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th class="m">Baptised</th><th>Child</th><th>Mother</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {d.weaver.map(([when, who, mo, note]) => (
            <tr style={/THE ONE/.test(note) ? "background:var(--accent-wash)" : ""}>
              <td class="m">{when}</td>
              <td><strong>{who}</strong></td>
              <td class="small">{mo}</td>
              <td class="small">{note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>The first child is baptised three months after the wedding</strong>, and the 1779 entry
      is the one time in thirteen that the clerk wrote the mother's name: <strong>Mary</strong>. That
      is the couple, and this is their family.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The correction</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Samuel is 1781, not 1769</h2>

    <p class="lede">The family tree says <strong>Samuel Sneyd, born 1769 at Madeley</strong>, son of
    William Sneyd and Mary Blackbourne. This archive searched for that baptism twice today and
    reported it missing both times.</p>

    <p style="margin-top:1rem"><strong>It is missing because it is not in 1769.</strong> The register's
    Samuel, son of William, is baptised <strong>4 June 1781</strong>. The year 1769 belongs to his
    eldest brother <em>William</em>, christened three months after the wedding — which is exactly the
    kind of slip a family tree makes when it has a marriage date and guesses the child from it.</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>And 1781 fits better.</strong> Samuel Charles Sneyd was born at Hanley in March 1811 to
      a father Samuel. A Samuel baptised in 1781 is thirty when that child arrives; one born in 1769
      would be forty-two. <span class="chip inf">identification, on dates and a documented family</span>
      <a href={u("/sneyd/")}>The line as it now stands →</a>
    </div>

    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>And the lesson, which is the third of its kind
    today.</strong> I searched for the tree's <em>date</em> instead of the tree's <em>person</em>. The
    1781 Samuel was in every result list I pulled, and I discarded him each time for being twelve
    years out. <a href={u("/corrections/")}>Recorded →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">A limit of the method</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Madeley does not write the mothers down</h2>
    <p>Of {c.madeleyBaptisms} Madeley baptisms in this set, only <strong>{c.motherRecorded}</strong>
    name the mother; <strong>{c.motherOmitted}</strong> give the father alone. Reconstitution works by
    grouping on both parents, so at Madeley it cannot separate two contemporary Williams — and the
    raw grouping duly produced one impossible "William" with thirty-four children spanning 1702 to
    1794.</p>
    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <strong>Which is why the weaver's family above is offered as a reading rather than a
      reconstruction.</strong> It hangs on an occupation that matches the marriage, a first child
      three months after it, and one entry in 1779 that names Mary. That is good — it is not the
      same as a register that writes both parents every time, as Dursley does.
      <a href={u("/sanigers/")}>Where it did work →</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">{d.fmp.census1851.ref}</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Samuel Sneyd, grocer, in his own shop</h2>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Relation</th><th class="m">Age</th><th>Occupation</th><th>Born</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {d.fmp.census1851.rows.map(([fn, sn, rel, ms, sx, age, by, occ, bp]) => (
            <tr style={fn === "Samuel" || fn === "Elizabeth" ? "background:var(--tint)" : ""}>
              <td><strong>{fn}</strong> <span class="small" style="color:var(--ink-3)">{sn}</span></td>
              <td class="small">{rel}</td><td class="m">{age}</td>
              <td class="small">{occ}</td><td class="small"><strong>{bp}</strong></td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>{d.fmp.census1851.means}</strong> <span class="chip doc">documented</span>
      <br /><br />
      {d.fmp.staly}
    </div>

    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1.2rem">{d.fmp.ages}</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Four censuses, one address</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">{d.wellStreetYears.head}</h2>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th class="m">Year</th><th>Address</th><th>Household</th><th class="m">Reference</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {d.wellStreetYears.rows.map(([y, addr, who, ref]) => (
            <tr><td class="m"><strong>{y}</strong></td><td><strong>{addr}</strong></td>
                <td class="small">{who}</td><td class="mono small">{ref}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>{d.wellStreetYears.means}</strong>
      <a href={u("/people/samuel-charles-sneyd-1810/")}>Samuel Charles →</a>
    </div>

    <p class="small" style="margin-top:1.2rem">{d.wellStreetYears.mullock}</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">{d.fmp.oliver.ref}</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">And Elizabeth's own baptism</h2>
    <p class="lede">{d.fmp.oliver.line}.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">{d.fmp.oliver.tree}</p>
    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      {d.fmp.oliver.reading} <span class="chip inf">an identification</span>
      <br /><br />
      <strong>{d.fmp.oliver.new}</strong>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Tested, covered, not found</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The Audlem marriage</h2>
    <p class="lede">{d.fmp.audlem.claim}</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem"><strong>The test.</strong> {d.fmp.audlem.test}</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem"><strong>The result.</strong> {d.fmp.audlem.result}</p>
    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">{d.fmp.audlem.verdict}
      <span class="chip doc">coverage established</span></div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Hanley St John the Evangelist · and the GRO index</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">91 Well Street, and the year that was wrong</h2>

    <p class="lede">The tree said: <em>{d.death.tree}</em> Nothing stood behind it. Here is the
    burial register.</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Register</td><td>{d.death.burial.reg}</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Buried</td><td class="m"><strong>{d.death.burial.date}</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Name</td><td><strong>{d.death.burial.who}</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Age</td><td class="m"><strong>{d.death.burial.age}</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Abode</td><td><strong>{d.death.burial.abode}</strong></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>And the death index agrees with the register, not the tree.</strong>
      {d.death.index.entry}
      <br /><br />
      {d.death.index.window} <span class="chip doc">documented</span>
    </div>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>What the tree said</th><th>What the records say</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {d.death.verdict.map(([k, t, r]) => (
            <tr><td><strong>{k}</strong></td><td class="small">{t}</td><td class="small">{r}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>The age is the interesting one.</strong> {d.death.age}
      <a href={u("/corrections/")}>The correction it supports →</a>
    </div>

    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1.2rem">{d.death.closing}</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">1861 census · {d.wellStreet.census}</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Well Street, Hanley — the grocer's shop</h2>

    <p class="lede">The family tree makes two very specific, entirely unverified claims about this
    line: that <a href={u("/people/samuel-sneyd/")}>Samuel Sneyd</a>, born Madeley 1769, "a grocer of
    Hanley", died on <strong>20 September 1856 at No 3, Well St, Hanley</strong>; and that he left a
    widow, <strong>Elizabeth Margaret Oliver of Oswestry, Shropshire</strong>, who outlived him by
    five years and died on 1 December 1861. No record behind either. Nobody had ever opened a
    Staffordshire census.</p>

    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem">Here is Well Street five years later.</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Relation</th><th class="m">Cond.</th><th class="m">Age</th><th>Occupation</th><th>Born</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {d.wellStreet.rows.map(([sn, fn, rel, cond, age, occ, cty, pl]) => (
            <tr>
              <td><strong>{fn}</strong> <span class="small" style="color:var(--ink-3)">{sn}</span></td>
              <td class="small">{rel === "MOTHER" ? <strong>Mother</strong> : rel}</td>
              <td class="m small">{cond}</td>
              <td class="m"><strong>{age}</strong></td>
              <td class="small">{occ || "—"}</td>
              <td class="small">{cty}{pl ? `, ${pl}` : ""}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:.7rem">The address in the enumerator's own hand:
      <strong>{d.wellStreet.address}</strong>.</p>

    <div class="lab" style="margin-top:1.6rem">Five points of contact</div>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:.6rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>What the tree says</th><th>What the census says</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {d.wellStreet.matches.map(([k, tree, cen]) => (
            <tr><td><strong>{k}</strong></td><td class="small">{tree || "—"}</td><td class="small">{cen}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>Read carefully.</strong> {d.wellStreet.caveat}
      <span class="chip inf">an identification, not a record</span>
      <br /><br />
      <strong>And one number does not fit.</strong> {d.wellStreet.ageProblem}
    </div>

    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1.2rem">What it does give outright is a household: a
    <strong>James Sneyd, engraver, born Hanley about 1819</strong>, with two daughters who paint
    pottery for a living. The tree gives Samuel and Elizabeth five children and this archive
    publishes only one of them —
    <a href={u("/people/samuel-charles-sneyd-1810/")}>Samuel Charles</a>, born Hanley 1810, who sailed.
    James stayed, kept the shop, and engraved.</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <strong>Her birthplace is left as it was written.</strong> {d.wellStreet.birthplace}
      <a href={u("/corrections/")}>Why that rule exists →</a>
    </div>

    <p class="small" style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>And why this took so long to find.</strong>
    {d.coverage1861} <span class="chip doc">coverage established by control test</span></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>Where the Staffordshire Sneyds are</h2>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.1rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Parish</th><th class="m">Records</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{d.places.map(([p, n]) => (
          <tr><td><strong>{p}</strong></td><td class="m">{n}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem">Madeley holds 149 of 551 — and Keele, the hall the discredited graft
    reached for, is four miles up the road and holds a handful. The family this archive actually
    descends from were <strong>weavers, carpenters, blacksmiths, brickmakers and labourers</strong>
    in the Potteries. <a href={u("/how-far-back/")}>The graft →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
